---
description: Bleed pulls content past its container's padding with negative margins, for full-width media and dividers inside padded panels.
---

<script>
  import { Bleed, Box, Stack, Text } from "carbon-components-svelte";
</script>

## Basic

Set `horizontal` and `vertical` to the same step as the surrounding `Box` padding. Bleed step `N` resolves to the negative of `Box` padding step `N`, so the content reaches the box's edges exactly.

<Box fill="layer-01" padding={5} border="subtle">
  <Bleed horizontal={5} vertical={5}>
    <Box fill="inverse" padding={7}>
      <Text type="body-short-01" color="inverse">Full bleed</Text>
    </Box>
  </Bleed>
</Box>

## Horizontal only

Set only `horizontal` to span the full width while keeping the vertical inset, for example a banner between padded paragraphs.

<Box fill="layer-01" padding={5} border="subtle">
  <Stack gap={5}>
    <Text type="body-long-01">Content above keeps its inset.</Text>
    <Bleed horizontal={5}>
      <Box fill="inverse" padding={5}>
        <Text type="body-short-01" color="inverse">Edge to edge</Text>
      </Box>
    </Bleed>
    <Text type="body-long-01">Content below keeps its inset.</Text>
  </Stack>
</Box>

## Per-side override

Set `top`, `right`, `bottom`, or `left` to override the axis value for one edge. Here `vertical={5}` bleeds top and bottom, and `top={0}` pins the top edge back inside the padding.

<Box fill="layer-01" padding={5} border="subtle">
  <Bleed horizontal={5} vertical={5} top={0}>
    <Box fill="inverse" padding={7}>
      <Text type="body-short-01" color="inverse">Top stays inset</Text>
    </Box>
  </Bleed>
</Box>

## Custom length

Strings accept any CSS length, including CSS variables and `calc()` expressions. The value is negated with `calc(-1 * (value))`.

<Box fill="layer-01" padding="1.25rem" border="subtle">
  <Bleed horizontal="1.25rem" vertical="1.25rem">
    <Box fill="inverse" padding={7}>
      <Text type="body-short-01" color="inverse">1.25rem bleed</Text>
    </Box>
  </Bleed>
</Box>

## Custom element

The bleed renders a `<div>` by default. Set `tag` to use a different element.

<Box fill="layer-01" padding={5} border="subtle">
  <Bleed tag="section" horizontal={5} vertical={5}>
    <Box fill="inverse" padding={7}>
      <Text type="body-short-01" color="inverse">Rendered as a `section`.</Text>
    </Box>
  </Bleed>
</Box>
